in the state of uttarakhand northern  india  a landslide has caused a deluge  it swept away bridges broke dams and  sent a torrent of debris and water  down a mountain valley dozens of bodies  have been recovered here  but the fear is over 100 are still  missing  but what exactly happened here and could  climate change be  to blame for this deadly incident  on sunday 7 february 2021  a landslide at the nanda devi glacier  caused a catastrophic event  a huge wall of water and debris plunged  through the chamoli district of  uttarakhand  northern india high up in the mountains  in nanda devi national park a large  crack had developed  on the side of one of the peaks this  caused an  enormous block of rock and ice to  collapse  into the valley the block was thought to  be over two million cubic meters in size  it fell nearly two kilometers to the  bottom of the valley  where it fragmented on impact and caused  a deluge  and when the flood reached here it  destroyed two dams that were filled with  workers  the flood damaged the rishiganga and  holy ganga hydropower projects  these images show the dam before and  after  part of the site was under construction  so there were workers everywhere  including in tunnels underneath the  plants  the eight metre high tunnel is packed  with debris and slush  and rescuers have been continually  removing earth  since operations began further inside  the tunnel  it gets even more difficult as the  height reduces to 3 meters  restricting bigger machinery hundreds of  people have been brought in for the  rescue efforts  including sniffer dogs digging and  clearing of the debris is taking place  in search of survivors and bodies over  the last 48 hours my boys are involved  all of us are involved we wanted to save  as many lives as we can  and in the bargain if you are able to  recover the dead bodies  it will be good for us workers are  trapped in tunnels like this one  some workers have been lucky enough to  be pulled alive  from the rubble but others were less  fortunate  satinegi and her elder sister have been  keeping a vigil  at the entrance since sunday her 30 year  old brother  was working as a supervisor when the  flood happened  my only brother younger brother we've  been waiting for him for three days now  we are praying that he returns safely  he's got two small daughters and our  mother at home  we have no father he's all that we have  so what exactly caused this if we  observe the  such events over the last two three  decades we can  clearly see the frequency of such events  is increasing in the himalayan region so  we can of course  link this with climate change however i  would stress  right now we we need more data  something similar happened in nepal in  2012  where a landslide caused the sati river  to flood  72 people died one reason behind these  disasters  is rising temperatures which causes the  ice to melt  and destabilizes the landscape and  basically you know it's previously  frozen landscapes  so we call it permafrost where the  grounds continue to be below  zero degrees so as this falls out and  this destabilizes these  steep slates um so you know chunks of  demand  are more likely to break through um but  you know the triggering mechanism of  this event is obviously still under  active investigation and it's  it's always difficult to pinpoint it  down to say this is climate change  these himalayan mountains have been  warming at an alarming rate  rapid large and unchecked construction  has precipitated this even  further affecting the fragile ecosystem  this is a warning which the government  may or may not pay  heed to but it's the vulnerable people  of the mountains  that will bear the brunt
 

